The rain didn’t fall so much as it *settled*, a fine, persistent mist that turned the city into a watercolor painting left out in the damp. I was sitting in the corner of a 24-hour laundromat, watching the dryers spin their slow, hypnotic circles. The air smelled of warm cotton, ozone, and the faint, metallic tang of old coins.

It was 3:17 AM. The kind of hour that doesn’t belong to the day or the night, but to the in-between. The kind of hour where the world feels thin.

I wasn’t there to do laundry. I was there to wait. Not for anything specific, really. Just to wait for the feeling to pass. The feeling that I had forgotten something important. A name. A face. A reason.

The dryer in front of me buzzed, then stopped with a soft *thump*. I didn’t move. I just watched the clothes tumble, a chaotic ballet of flannel and denim, folding and unfolding themselves into new shapes. It was oddly comforting. The randomness of it. The way the fabric found its own order, even if it was just for a moment.

A woman walked in. She was older, with silver hair pulled back in a loose bun, wearing a coat that looked like it had seen better decades. She didn’t look at me. She just walked to the far corner, where a single washer was still running, and sat on the plastic chair beside it. She pulled a book from her bag. Not a phone. A book. Paper. Ink.

I watched her open it. She didn’t read. She just held it in her lap, her fingers tracing the spine. Her eyes were closed. She looked like she was listening to something I couldn’t hear.

I felt a strange pull. Not curiosity, exactly. More like recognition. Like I had seen this scene before, in a dream, or in a life I hadn’t lived yet.

I stood up. My legs felt stiff. I walked over to her. I didn’t say anything. I just sat down on the chair next to hers. The plastic was cold.

She opened her eyes. She looked at me. Her gaze was clear, sharp, but not unkind.

"You’re waiting," she said. It wasn’t a question.

"I think so," I said.

"For what?"

I shook my head. "I don’t know. I just feel like I’m supposed to be here."

She smiled. It was a small, sad smile. "We all are, at some point. Waiting for the next thing to make sense."

She turned a page in her book. "What’s the title?" I asked, nodding at the book.

She looked down. "‘The Unfinished Map’."

I frowned. "What’s it about?"

"About the places we haven’t gone yet," she said. "And the ones we’ve already left behind. And how the map is never finished. It’s always being redrawn."

I looked at the dryers. The clothes were still tumbling. The mist was still falling. The city was still sleeping.

"I think I forgot my name," I said. It came out quiet, almost to myself.

She didn’t look up. "Does it matter?"

"I don’t know. I just feel like I’m missing a piece."

She closed the book. "Maybe the piece isn’t missing. Maybe it’s just not written yet."

I looked at her. "How do you know that?"

She shrugged. "I’ve been waiting a long time. I’ve learned that the waiting isn’t the problem. The problem is thinking the waiting is the end."

I sat there, in the cold plastic chair, in the warm, smelly air of the laundromat, and I felt something shift. Not a solution. Not an answer. Just a shift. A small, quiet turning.

The dryer buzzed again. Then stopped.

I stood up. I didn’t know where I was going. I didn’t know my name. But I knew I wasn’t going to stay in the corner anymore.

I walked to the door. I pushed it open. The mist rushed in, cool and wet. The city was still there, blurred and beautiful and indifferent.

I stepped out.

I didn’t look back.

I just walked. [end of text]


